---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Installation, ground slab and structural design for lab containers – the practice guide",
  "description": "Ground conditions, load assumptions, foundation variants and crane logistics for lab containers: DIN EN 1997, Eurocode 1, DIN 1054, snow-load zones, wind zones, frost depth, pad/strip/slab foundations, screw piles and abnormal-load logistics – with verified standards.",
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Sven Biewald", "url": "https://planexus.de/en/team/sven-biewald", "jobTitle": "Managing Director", "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Planexus GmbH" } },
  "datePublished": "2026-05-14",
  "dateModified": "2026-05-14",
  "image": "https://planexus.de/attached_assets/generated_images/laborcontainer-aufstellung-kran-bodenplatte.png",
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Planexus GmbH", "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://planexus.de/images/planexus-logo.png" } },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://planexus.de/en/magazine/lab-container-installation-foundation-statics" }
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://planexus.de/en" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Magazine", "item": "https://planexus.de/en/magazine" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Installation, ground slab and structural design", "item": "https://planexus.de/en/magazine/lab-container-installation-foundation-statics" }
  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Does a lab container always need a ground slab?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. A continuous reinforced-concrete ground slab is only one of four common foundation variants. For most lab containers of up to two modules, pad foundations under the container corners suffice (four to eight pads per module, concrete C25/30, frost-free to DIN EN ISO 13793). Strip foundations under the longitudinal beams are the next step. A ground slab becomes mandatory on very soft or inhomogeneous ground, on cleanroom or GMP applications with high settlement sensitivity, on large modular assemblies, or when the container still needs continuous load distribution above the ground. Which variant fits is decided by the ground investigation to DIN EN ISO 14688 and the load assumption to Eurocode 1 (DIN EN 1991)." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which standards apply to the structural design of a lab container?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The stability proof rests on four principal standards. Eurocode 0 (DIN EN 1990) supplies the basis of structural design and defines the design situations. Eurocode 1 (DIN EN 1991) governs actions – self-weight, imposed loads, snow to DIN EN 1991-1-3 with the five German snow-load zones (1, 1a, 2, 2a, 3), wind to DIN EN 1991-1-4 with the four wind zones. Eurocode 7 (DIN EN 1997-1) governs geotechnical design. In Germany, DIN 1054:2021-04 additionally applies as the National Annex with the supplementary rules for EC 7 application. For the building submission the structural engineer additionally checks the state building code – who may sign the stability proof is regulated by federal state (authorisation to submit building documents)." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How deep must a container foundation be taken for frost?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "In Germany generally 80 centimetres, in upland and high locations (Black Forest, Bavarian Forest, Ore Mountains above 600 m) 100 to 150 centimetres. What governs are the regional frost maps of the state building-control authorities; DIN EN ISO 13793 supplies the thermal design of frost protection in the foundation zone. Anyone who undershoots the frost depth risks frost heave – freezing soil water expands by about 9 per cent and causes uneven lifting. On screw piles frost freedom is inherent in the construction through the screw length." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which snow and wind loads must the container structural engineer apply?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Snow load is measured to DIN EN 1991-1-3 including the National Annex. Germany is divided into five zones: Zone 1 (North German Plain), 1a (west/south transition areas), 2 (upland foothills), 2a (eastern Bavaria, Saxony) and 3 (high locations, Alpine foothills). Characteristic snow load at ground level (sk) varies from around 0.65 kN/m² in Zone 1 (at heights under 400 m) to over 4.0 kN/m² in high locations of Zone 3. For a container roof, shape and exposure coefficients are additionally applied. Wind load to DIN EN 1991-1-4 uses the four German wind zones (1 to 4) with characteristic basic velocity vb,0 of 22.5 m/s in Zone 1 to 30 m/s in Zone 4 (North Sea and Baltic coast). For stability, the suction component on the roof and the uplift force at corners are particularly relevant – on light containers they decide the anchorage." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Screw pile or concrete foundation – when which?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Screw piles (helical piles, Krinner, Sun Tower or comparable type) are the fast variant: screwed in with a hydraulic rotary head in two to three hours per container, no excavation, no curing, immediately loadable, removable without construction waste. Worthwhile for temporary installations, urgent projects (pandemic laboratories, construction-site use), nature-conservation areas and contaminated sites where excavation is to be avoided. Prerequisite: adequate bearing capacity of the soil after a trial borehole, no rock at shallow depth. Concrete foundations (pad, strip, slab) are the permanent variant and, for GMP/cleanroom applications, usually without alternative: higher stiffness, lower settlements, longer service life (typically 50+ years versus 25 years for screws), unrestricted load reserves for later stacking. Premium of concrete versus screw: typically a factor of 2 to 4 in material and construction time." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which crane is needed to install a lab container?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "It depends on the container weight and the required boom radius. A fully fitted-out 20-foot lab container weighs between eight and twelve tonnes, a 40-foot container or a double module between 14 and 24 tonnes. For most installations a mobile crane of the 50 to 100 tonne lifting-capacity class (Liebherr LTM 1050, LTM 1070, LTM 1090) at a boom radius of 8 to 16 metres suffices. In confined conditions or over long reaches, larger cranes (LTM 1200, LTM 1300, occasionally LTM 1500) are used. What governs is DGUV Regulation 52 (cranes) and DGUV Rule 100-500 Chapter 2.8. Before every installation we produce a lift and standing plan together with the crane contractor, check the crane pad for bearing capacity (generally 150 to 250 kN/m² under the outriggers) and secure the traffic to RSA 21." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How does delivery run – abnormal load or ordinary HGV?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Up to an overall width of 2.55 metres, overall height 4.00 metres and a permissible total weight of the articulated combination of 40 tonnes, delivery with an ordinary articulated trailer is possible without a special permit – that covers standard lab containers in 20-foot construction. As soon as a container becomes wider (over 3.00 metres), higher (over 4.00 metres) or heavier (for example with built-in heavy-duty air-conditioning or with generous interior fit-out over 25 tonnes complete weight), §29 paragraph 3 StVZO applies and a large and heavy transport (GST) with a special permit becomes necessary – often with escort vehicles (BF3 or police) and night routing. We coordinate the transport concept including permit, escort and delivery time window with you before production release." } }
  ]
};
import RelatedArticles from '../../../components/RelatedArticles.astro';
