    <section class="py-16 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mb-8">Frequently asked questions on installation and structural design</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group" open>
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Does a lab container always need a ground slab?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">No. A ground slab is only one of four foundation variants. For most containers, <strong>pad foundations</strong> under the container corners (four to eight pads per module, concrete C25/30, frost-free to DIN EN ISO 13793) or <strong>strip foundations</strong> under the longitudinal beams suffice. A continuous reinforced-concrete ground slab becomes mandatory on cleanroom/GMP applications, on very settlement-sensitive soil and on large modular assemblies. Which variant fits is decided by the ground investigation to DIN EN ISO 14688 and the load assumption to Eurocode 1.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Which standards apply to the structural design of a lab container?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Four principal standards: <strong>DIN EN 1990</strong> (Eurocode 0, basis of structural design), <strong>DIN EN 1991</strong> (Eurocode 1, actions – incl. 1991-1-3 snow and 1991-1-4 wind), <strong>DIN EN 1997-1</strong> (Eurocode 7, geotechnics) and <strong>DIN 1054:2021-04</strong> as the German National Annex. For the building submission the structural engineer additionally checks the state building code – who may sign the stability proof is regulated by federal state in the authorisation to submit building documents.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">How deep must a container foundation be taken for frost?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">In Germany generally <strong>80 cm</strong>, in upland and high locations (Black Forest, Bavarian Forest, Ore Mountains above 600 m) <strong>100 to 150 cm</strong>. What governs is <strong>DIN EN ISO 13793</strong> and the regional frost maps. Anyone who undershoots the frost depth risks <strong>frost heave</strong> – freezing soil water expands by about 9 % and causes uneven lifting. On screw piles frost freedom is inherent in the construction through the screw length.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Which snow and wind loads must the container structural engineer apply?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Snow to <strong>DIN EN 1991-1-3</strong>: Germany in five zones (1, 1a, 2, 2a, 3), characteristic ground snow load sk from around 0.65 kN/m² (Zone 1, &lt;400 m) to &gt;4.0 kN/m² (Zone 3 high locations). Wind to <strong>DIN EN 1991-1-4</strong>: four wind zones, vb,0 from 22.5 m/s (Zone 1) to 30.0 m/s (Zone 4, North Sea/Baltic). Practice-relevant besides the pressure component is above all the suction component on the roof and the uplift force at the container corners – they decide the anchorage.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Screw pile or concrete foundation – when which?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed"><strong>Screw piles</strong>: fast (installation on the same day), no excavation, removable – ideal for temporary installations, urgent projects, nature conservation, contaminated sites. Service life 25–30 years. <strong>Concrete foundations</strong> (pad, strip, slab): higher stiffness, lower settlements, 50+ years service life, mandatory for cleanroom/GMP. Premium of concrete versus screw: typically a factor of 2 to 4 in material and construction time.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">Which crane is needed to install a lab container?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Depends on container weight and boom radius. 20-foot lab container: 8–12 t. 40-foot container/double module: 14–24 t. Standard solution: <strong>mobile crane 50 to 100 t</strong> (Liebherr LTM 1050, LTM 1070, LTM 1090) at 8–16 m radius. In confined conditions or a long boom radius up to 200–300 t (LTM 1200, LTM 1300). What governs: <strong>DGUV Regulation 52</strong> and <strong>DGUV Rule 100-500 Chapter 2.8</strong>. In advance: lift and standing plan, bearing-capacity check of the outrigger areas (typically 150–250 kN/m²), traffic management to RSA 21.</div>
            </details>
            <details class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden group">
              <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-900 hover:text-primary transition-colors">How does delivery run – abnormal load or ordinary HGV?
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 text-primary transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
              </summary>
              <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Up to 2.55 m overall width, 4.00 m overall height and 40 t combination total weight: ordinary articulated trailer without a special permit. Beyond that: <strong>large and heavy transport (GST)</strong> to <strong>§29 para. 3 StVZO</strong> with a special permit from the road-traffic authority, escort vehicles (BF3 or police), defined routing and usually a night delivery time window. Lead time for the permit: 4 to 8 weeks per federal state.</div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
